      ":question/id": "38389750-0e07-56c4-a5aa-678aeeb21e2e",
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(9\\right)$ as a single logarithm. Assume $q>0$ and $p>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the product rule, combine the logarithms with the same sign. Then use the quotient rule to subtract the denominator logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\right)-\\ln\\left(9\\right) &= \\ln\\left(q\\right)-\\ln\\left(p\\cdot 9\\right) \\\\ &= \\ln\\left(\\frac{q}{9\\cdot p}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
